The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ctl & DMA_CTL_STATUS_OWN)
    break;
```
```c
if (handled < weight) {
    napi_complete_done(napi, handled);
    bcm4908_enet_intrs_on(enet);
}
```
Reason:
The first statement checks if the descriptor is owned by the hardware, and if so, it breaks out of the loop without processing further packets. If the hardware runs out of free descriptors, it may stop the RX ring, leading to a situation where no further packets can be received. The second statement completes the NAPI polling without re-enabling the RX ring, which means that if the RX ring was disabled due to a lack of descriptors, it remains disabled.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bcm4908_enet_dma_rx_ring_enable(enet, &enet->rx_ring);
```
Reason:
This statement explicitly re-enables the RX ring after processing the received packets. By adding this line, the code ensures that even if the hardware runs out of descriptors and disables the RX ring, it will be re-enabled after handling the packets, allowing for continued packet reception.

